<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>New distortion and a central blurred spot in the left eye of an 80-year-old painter, suspicious for neovascular age-related macular degeneration. Please evaluate this week and treat as indicated. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Urgent, within the week, because of possible choroidal neovascularization. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Is this neovascular age-related macular degeneration, and should anti-VEGF treatment start now?</item><item>What is the state of the right eye, and should she take an AREDS2 supplement to protect it?</item><item>How should she monitor each eye at home, and what should prompt a same-day call?</item><item>Would low-vision rehabilitation help her keep painting, and is she safe to continue driving short daytime routes?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. O&apos;Keeffe is seen at the request of Dr. Ashby two days after her primary care visit. Over the past few weeks straight lines on her canvases and door frames have looked bent with the left eye, and small print and fine color differences have become hard to judge. The right eye seems unchanged. She has had no eye pain, redness, flashes, or new floaters. She takes no medicines and no blood thinners. She paints daily, drives short routes by day, and is afraid of losing the central vision she needs for her work. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Georgia Totto O&apos;Keeffe was born on November 15, 1887, to Francis Calyxtus O&apos;Keeffe and Ida Totto O&apos;Keeffe at the family dairy farm near Sun Prairie, Wisconsin, the second of seven children. [D]</item><item>O&apos;Keeffe&apos;s mother died in Charlottesville, Virginia, in May 1916, and her father died in Petersburg, Virginia, in November 1918. [D]</item><item>In 1927 O&apos;Keeffe was admitted twice to Mount Sinai Hospital in New York for surgery to remove benign breast cysts. [D]</item><item>In February 1933 O&apos;Keeffe was admitted to Doctor&apos;s Hospital in New York, suffering from psychoneurosis, and recuperated in Bermuda in March and April. [D]</item><item>O&apos;Keeffe&apos;s eyesight began to fail in 1968. [D]</item><item>The first signs of O&apos;Keeffe&apos;s macular degeneration may have appeared as early as 1964. [R]</item></list><paragraph>4 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

