<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Neovascular age-related macular degeneration, left eye, active (H35.3221). Confirmed clinically. A subfoveal gray-green lesion with subretinal fluid on OCT (central subfield thickness 418 µm) and acuity of 55 letters fit active choroidal neovascularization. The picture is typical, so treatment began today on OCT and fundus findings without fluorescein or OCT angiography; angiography is held in reserve if the response to loading is poor or the picture becomes atypical. The first intravitreal aflibercept 2 mg injection was given today without complication. [S]</item><item>Intermediate dry age-related macular degeneration, right eye (H35.3112). Large drusen and pigment changes without fluid or hemorrhage. This eye is at risk of conversion and now carries most of her reading and driving vision, so the AREDS2 supplement is indicated. [S]</item><item>Visual function and safety. She has trouble with small print and fine color judgment but remains independent. Right-eye acuity of 78 letters supports short local daytime driving for now, pending a formal driving assessment. Her mood is steady, but vision loss raises the risk of depression. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Today&apos;s optical coherence tomography of both maculae, best-corrected acuity and pressures, and the primary care Amsler grid findings and acuity from two days ago. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Intravitreal aflibercept 2 mg in the left eye every four weeks for three loading doses, then treat-and-extend by two-week steps while the macula stays dry; first dose given today. [S]</item><item>At each injection visit: best-corrected acuity, OCT, intraocular pressure, and dilated examination of the right eye. [S]</item><item>Fluorescein or OCT angiography only if the response to loading is poor or the findings become atypical. [S]</item><item>AREDS2 supplement daily for the right eye (vitamin C, vitamin E, lutein, zeaxanthin, zinc, and copper; no beta-carotene), started today. [S]</item><item>Daily Amsler grid checks of each eye in turn, with a same-day call for any new distortion or blur in either eye. [S]</item><item>Call the same day for eye pain, increasing redness, worsening vision, light sensitivity, or a curtain over the vision; large-print instructions given. [S]</item><item>Low-vision rehabilitation, with a home fall-risk review and a formal driving assessment; referral sent today. Until then, short local daytime routes only. [S]</item><item>Repeat the PHQ-2 at the next primary care review. [S]</item><item>Letter to Dr. Ashby today. The diagnosis, the injection schedule, and the warning signs were discussed with the patient, who agreed to treatment. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Retina clinic in four weeks for acuity, OCT, pressure, and the second injection, and at each injection visit after that. Primary care will repeat the PHQ-2 and review the rehabilitation and driving outcome at the first treat-and-extend visit. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
